From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: a console reads its trackpad through libinput

Patch 0026 put a pointer on the screen and the trackpad still did not
move it. This is why, and it is the sixth time this shape has appeared.

A CORRECTION TO PATCH 0026 FIRST. That patch's message says "the
trackpad works and always did", on the evidence that
`the first pointer event reached the window` was logged before the first
key. That inference was wrong. Patch 0025 gates that line on
`event->IsLocatedEvent()`, and two things produce a located event at
startup with no trackpad involved: `DrmCursor::CommitBoundsChange` ->
`DrmWindowHost::SynthesizeMouseMove` ->
`EventFactoryEvdev::SynthesizeMouseMove`
(`ui/ozone/platform/drm/host/drm_cursor.cc:152`,
`ui/ozone/platform/drm/host/drm_window_host.cc:150`,
`ui/events/ozone/evdev/event_factory_evdev.cc:578`), which patch 0018's
fullscreen `SetBoundsInPixels` drives on every startup; and a click on
the pad, which is a `BTN_LEFT` key event dispatched at
`cursor_->GetLocation()`. Neither is motion.

NOTHING IN THIS TREE TURNS A TOUCHPAD INTO POINTER MOTION. The converter
a device gets is chosen by `CreateConverter`
(`ui/events/ozone/evdev/input_device_opener_evdev.cc:42-103`), and its
touchpad branch is `#if defined(USE_EVDEV_GESTURES)` -- the ChromeOS
gestures library, whose gn flag is `use_evdev_gestures =
is_chromeos_device` (`ui/events/ozone/evdev/BUILD.gn:15`). A laptop pad
is not a touchscreen either: `HasTouchscreen()` is
`HasAbsXY() && HasDirect()` and `INPUT_PROP_POINTER` makes `HasDirect()`
false (`ui/events/ozone/evdev/event_device_info.cc:898,1019`). So it
falls through to `EventConverterEvdevImpl`, whose `ProcessEvents`
handles `EV_MSC`, `EV_KEY`, `EV_REL`, `EV_SYN` and `EV_SW`
(`event_converter_evdev_impl.cc:215-247`) and has no `EV_ABS` case at
all. A pad's `ABS_MT_*` are read off the descriptor and dropped;
`x_offset_` and `y_offset_` stay zero; `FlushEvents` returns at its
first line (`:397`) and never reaches
`cursor_->MoveCursor` on the next (`:400`). Nothing logs, because
nothing failed. Ozone/drm's own cursor plumbing is complete and
platform-neutral, and was never the problem: `DrmCursor::OnWindowAdded`
is called from `DrmWindowHost::Initialize()` for every platform
(`drm_window_host.cc:56`).

The fifth instance was `BitmapCursorFactory`; this is the sixth, and the
ash-only piece is in `//ui/events/ozone/evdev` rather than
`//ui/ozone/platform/drm`.

SO THE TOUCHPAD GOES TO libinput, which upstream already has a converter
for and which this build can have for a gn argument:
`libinput-dev` and `libinput10` are in Chromium's own bullseye sysroot
(`build/linux/sysroot_scripts/generated_package_lists/bullseye.amd64`),
so `use_libinput = true` needs no sysroot surgery. Tap, two-finger
scroll, acceleration and palm rejection come with it, maintained by
people who do that for a living, and the alternative was writing a
gesture engine in this repository.

AND THE ONE THING THAT STOPS IT WORKING HERE IS THE DESCRIPTOR. Every
other branch of `CreateConverter` is handed the `fd` the opener already
obtained; the libinput branch dropped it on the floor and let libinput
open the path itself, through an `open_restricted` that was a bare
`open(path, flags)`. On a console this browser is not root and owns no
device node -- that is the whole reason patch 0020 exists, and
`DrmLogindInput::OpenDeviceFd` is where a descriptor comes from. An
`open()` by name there answers EACCES and the pad is simply never
opened.

So `LibInputContext::Create` now takes the descriptor, parks it where
`OpenRestricted` can find it, and hands it over instead of opening
anything. The park is a heap allocation and not a member address on
purpose: libinput keeps the pointer as its user data for the life of the
context, and the context is moved on its way out of `Create`, so
anything addressed relative to `this` would dangle by the time libinput
asked for it. One device per context is this class's own documented
rule, so there is exactly one descriptor to give and a second ask is a
bug -- it answers `-EACCES` and says so, rather than falling back to the
`open()` that cannot work.

AND THIS FILE HAD NEVER BEEN COMPILED. `use_libinput` was only ever set
by ChromeOS device builds, so turning it on here put
`libinput_event_converter.{h,cc}` in front of two checks it had never
met, and the first build failed on eleven errors, none of them in code
this patch wrote:

  chromium-rawptr                            5 pointer members
  -Wunsafe-buffer-usage-in-format-attr-call  3 `base::StringPrintV` calls

Both are fixed rather than switched off. The pointer members become
`raw_ptr`, which costs nothing on handles libinput allocated and frees
-- the protection it adds applies to memory this process allocated --
and the two that are Chromium's own, the dispatcher and the cursor, get
what every other pointer member in this directory has. The log handler's
format calls are wrapped in `UNSAFE_BUFFERS` with the reason: `format`
and `args` are libinput's own, its contract is a null-terminated printf
format and the arguments that go with it, and the callback is reachable
from nowhere else. The alternative was
`configs -= [ "//build/config/clang:find_bad_constructs" ]`, which would
have turned the plugin off for all of `//ui/events/ozone/evdev` to avoid
five conversions.

AND THEN THE BUILD RAN ONE OF ITS OWN BINARIES. `use_libinput = true`
links libinput into `libcontent.so`, and `v8_context_snapshot_generator`
is a host tool the build LINKS AND THEN EXECUTES. The link was fine --
the sysroot has the library -- and the exec was not:

  ././v8_context_snapshot_generator: error while loading shared
  libraries: libinput.so.10: cannot open shared object file

`tools/nix/make-shell-for-system.nix` is the build shell, and it already
carries a list of libraries headed by that exact error text and that
exact binary's name: X11, mesa, libgbm, libdrm, alsa, pango, dbus. A
library the browser links is a library the build has to be able to load.
So libinput joins the list it was always going to belong to, one line
and a reason.

Routing is a runtime flag rather than a patch:
`EventDeviceInfo::UseLibinput` honors an overridden
`kLibinputHandleTouchpad` above its own heuristics
(`event_device_info.cc:792-806`), and `domicile-launch` passes
`--enable-features=LibinputHandleTouchpad`. That keeps the decision
where a person can see and change it.
---
diff --git a/tools/nix/make-shell-for-system.nix b/tools/nix/make-shell-for-system.nix
index c87e1b5..69372e1 100644
--- a/tools/nix/make-shell-for-system.nix
+++ b/tools/nix/make-shell-for-system.nix
@@ -96,6 +96,13 @@ in
       xorg.libXfixes # libXfixes.so.3
       xorg.libXrandr # libXrandr.so.2
       xorg.libXtst # libXtst.so.6
+      # libinput.so.10, which `use_libinput = true` links into libcontent.so
+      # and so into every host binary the build runs. The list above is this
+      # one's own precedent: a generator that links the browser's libraries
+      # needs them present to EXECUTE, not merely to link against, and the
+      # sysroot that satisfied the linker is not on the loader's path. See
+      # patch 0027.
+      libinput # libinput.so.10
       mesa
       libgbm # libgbm.so.1
       libdrm # libdrm.so.2
diff --git a/ui/events/ozone/evdev/input_device_opener_evdev.cc b/ui/events/ozone/evdev/input_device_opener_evdev.cc
index e9b64c7..93c5dd1 100644
--- a/ui/events/ozone/evdev/input_device_opener_evdev.cc
+++ b/ui/events/ozone/evdev/input_device_opener_evdev.cc
@@ -48,8 +48,13 @@ std::unique_ptr<EventConverterEvdev> CreateConverter(
 #if defined(USE_LIBINPUT)
   // Use LibInputEventConverter for odd touchpads
   if (devinfo.UseLibinput()) {
-    return LibInputEventConverter::Create(params.path, params.id, devinfo,
-                                          params.cursor, params.dispatcher);
+    // THE DESCRIPTOR GOES WITH IT. Every other converter below is handed the
+    // `fd` this function was called with; this branch used to drop it and let
+    // libinput open the path itself, which on a console is the one thing that
+    // cannot work -- see `LibInputContext::OpenRestricted`.
+    return LibInputEventConverter::Create(std::move(fd), params.path, params.id,
+                                          devinfo, params.cursor,
+                                          params.dispatcher);
   }
 #endif
 
diff --git a/ui/events/ozone/evdev/libinput_event_converter.cc b/ui/events/ozone/evdev/libinput_event_converter.cc
index cfe335e..160a98c 100644
--- a/ui/events/ozone/evdev/libinput_event_converter.cc
+++ b/ui/events/ozone/evdev/libinput_event_converter.cc
